{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1024365-south-american-magick-traditions-and-so-on","id":1024365,"title":"South american magick, traditions and so on.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-12-30T09:01:08-08:00","id":1024365,"title":"South american magick, traditions and so on.","content":"i been curious on the different takes on cultural magick. Mainly on Chilean magic.<br /> im curious on how to adapt the Chilote Warlock ( <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warlock_of_Chilo%C3%A9\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warlock_of_Chilo%C3%A9</a> ) on WoD. <a href=\"http://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/into-the-cave-of-chiles-witches-20138093/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.smithsonianmag.com/histor...ches-20138093/</a><br /> <br /> According to the myths, they are capable of flying by dettaching their heads from their bodies and fly with their ears. Flying for one block and so on. they seem like mages from the dark ages on the contemporany age...but im pondering how would the paradox operate on so blatant and vulgar magic?..would the local Belief deny the paradox?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Forum's Official's Joker and Trickster. Pardon my bad english, aint my first language (I Speak Spanish).<br /> ST: DtF, HtR, WtO, MtA<br /> Signature Chars: Crowley (hakalu), Joe The Nuwisha (WtA)<br /> Changelings: be afraid of the Technocracian High Five of Doom","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1754-crowley","name":"Crowley","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1754_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-30T10:33:31-08:00","id":1024393,"title":"","content":"Local belief always matters and could make it coincidental even, as long as there's no pesky tourist around.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-30T10:45:14-08:00","id":1024397,"title":"","content":"Concerning this part of the world, I've mainly been preoccupied with Central America. Mexico alone could, these days, have Mages joining the Celestial Chorus (those who blend traditional practices with Catholicism), the Verbana (for more &quot;vanilla&quot; native practices), the Dreamspeakers (for those native practices that skew more towards the spirit worlds), and the Euthanatoi (for followers of Santa Muerte, who has only grown <i>more popular</i> in the public consciousness instead of less). This of course not even including the various Caribbean and southern US practices, resulting from African slaves mixing their beliefs with native and European ideas.<br /> <br /> No, White Wolf, the magical landscape of Central and South America is not solely limited to what the Aztecs, Maya, and Inca were doing. Indeed, there's a thriving undercurrent of mysticism in that part of the world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-31T14:01:24-08:00","id":1024891,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1024365-south-american-magick-traditions-and-so-on?p=1024397#post1024397\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Concerning this part of the world, I've mainly been preoccupied with Central America. Mexico alone could, these days, have Mages joining the Celestial Chorus (those who blend traditional practices with Catholicism), the Verbana (for more &quot;vanilla&quot; native practices), the Dreamspeakers (for those native practices that skew more towards the spirit worlds), and the Euthanatoi (for followers of Santa Muerte, who has only grown <i>more popular</i> in the public consciousness instead of less). This of course not even including the various Caribbean and southern US practices, resulting from African slaves mixing their beliefs with native and European ideas.<br /> <br /> No, White Wolf, the magical landscape of Central and South America is not solely limited to what the Aztecs, Maya, and Inca were doing. Indeed, there's a thriving undercurrent of mysticism in that part of the world.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not sure what &quot;vanilla&quot; Verbena practices would mean. Verbena aren't really very &quot;vanilla&quot; except in a very sort of plush-huggable-New Age kind of way. Blood, spit, sacrifice, potions, etc. Not vanilla. In fact there are some fairly disturbing customs in the Chiapas that seem to go straight from Mayan religious customs to modern versions that might make a lot of sense as Verbena Instruments... the combination with lucadore masks (with the most common being those of jaguars) with a tradition of purposefully bloody fist-fights among young men in a set of villages around harvest time. The bloodier th efight the better. Shedding more blood is more important than winning. Or the nearby village where they mark the same time with a pilgrimage of children down a cenote to the waterline and then back out. Which doesn't sound so bad until you know they have found evidence of multiple incidents of child sacrifice by the Mayans in the same waters... Of course, whether those are &quot;Verbena&quot; or not depends on how Eurocentric the Verbena currently are. They certainly would have been consigned to &quot;Dreamspeaker-stuff&quot; back in the day.<br /> <br /> The Cult of Santa Muerte has a HUGE Aztec component and probably moreso in a WOD slant, as does much of the more &quot;death/dead friendly&quot; side of Mexican culture that results in Dia de Muertos, though there are definitely some Mayan, Zapotec, etc. aspects in there as well. If there's a Euthanatos group in there, it's not one that's gotten a lot of attention in the past. Though it might be a really fertile area for one. Or, perhaps some allied Euthanatos, Dreamspeakers, Verbena and maybe Choristers who all borrow from a rich combination of indigenous and European occult lore. It almost seems like it could almost be a Craft of some sort.... like the Carribean melange that results in the Bataa. Not really sure what to do with Jesus Malverde in all that......","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-31T14:04:37-08:00","id":1024895,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crowley</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1024365-south-american-magick-traditions-and-so-on#post1024365\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">i been curious on the different takes on cultural magick. Mainly on Chilean magic.<br /> im curious on how to adapt the Chilote Warlock ( <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warlock_of_Chilo%C3%A9\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warlock_of_Chilo%C3%A9</a> ) on WoD. <a href=\"http://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/into-the-cave-of-chiles-witches-20138093/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.smithsonianmag.com/histor...ches-20138093/</a><br /> <br /> According to the myths, they are capable of flying by dettaching their heads from their bodies and fly with their ears. Flying for one block and so on. they seem like mages from the dark ages on the contemporany age...but im pondering how would the paradox operate on so blatant and vulgar magic?..would the local Belief deny the paradox?</div> </div> </div> </div>Perhaps they wouldn't be mages? Sounds like some kind of indigenous supernatural very similar to Indonesian penanggalan that may have started out as a human, but.... isn't, anymore. Do these guys have to stay human or could they have undergone conversion to &quot;something else&quot;?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-31T15:16:14-08:00","id":1024918,"title":"","content":"Every single Tradition or Craft is, to an extend, Dark Ages mages trying to live in the contemporary world. Deal with Paradox when your Paradigm is vulgar is the bread and meat of Mage. Even for most Technocrats it is this way.<br /> <br /> So, those Mages will mostly have to deal with the same problems as everyone: they can evade Paradox through enough weight in their favor in the local Tellurian creating a similar Pattern. They can have their own shrines, spaces where they defined a new dominant Paradigm (theirs), they can try to disguise those effects. Or they can simply accept that Paradox is a thing in their lives.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-31T17:39:07-08:00","id":1024953,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1024365-south-american-magick-traditions-and-so-on?p=1024895#post1024895\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Perhaps they wouldn't be mages? Sounds like some kind of indigenous supernatural very similar to Indonesian penanggalan that may have started out as a human, but.... isn't, anymore. Do these guys have to stay human or could they have undergone conversion to &quot;something else&quot;?</div> </div> </div> </div>they are humans. But they have particular weaknesses. If they sit down on a chair with a cross bound to it, they can't stand up. They cant eat salt (since its holy).<br /> <br /> As for their powers. They come in different &quot;Flavours.&quot; But imma try to writte down some of the things they can do.<br /> -Shapeshift: Cat, Dogs, Flying Head/Tue-Tue, Its believed that they can hear you on your house, they fly while saying the word Tue-Tue. The only way to keep them away is by burning salt or by having the skull of a beloved horse on the house. IF you call out the Tue-Tue like saying &quot;Come to my place tomorrow and ill make you dinner.&quot; the Warlock WILL show up and wont leave until you do what you said you would do (Such as, making dinner for him). IF you turn their body down to earth (while their head is flying) they cant come back to their bodies nor they can re-attach themselves. They can't attack if you're facing them, but if you turn their back on them , they can use their spells against ye<br /> <br /> <br /> -Making Sleep: They can make their victim's fall asleep just by knowing their First Name. they go inside the victim's house and they use the Claw of an Owl to cut the victim's body and remove his scalp to create Sickness Powders.<br /> <br /> -Cursing: Yes, they can do that.<br /> <br /> -Reviving people: apparently they can bring someone back to life within their Caves<br /> <br /> -They must do a Dark Baptsim to renounce to God, for this they must be baptized infront of a Demon<br /> <br /> -They Can cause Desease and bring &quot;Back Luck&quot; to those getting too close to them.<br /> <br /> -They use a lot of herbs and plants, animals guts and bones in order to cause vomits and diarreah to their targets. as well to heal them.<br /> <br /> -They Can Fly but just for a block from distance and they can carry someone while doing this.<br /> <br /> The main way to keep them at bay is by praying to god or by using holy symbols. Such as the Cross/Star of Solomon. By burning salt, the Warlock will fall in sick and die within a year. If the Warlock is flying on his Tue Tue form, he will fall but you must pray to a Saint (saint benito) in order to keep him on the ground.<br /> <br /> -They usually fly to meet up, if they need to travel, they may summon a ghost ship (El Caleuche) which turns into a piece of wood by day, by night it claims the souls of the curious to turn it into slaves.<br /> <br /> -They use a cloak made of dead man's skin (chest to be precise), with it they can open doors, go invisible, create light, fly and so on<br /> <br /> They are somehow secretive. they are a sort of &quot;secret&quot;, even if everyone on the town knows who the local warlock is. they dont mess with him out of fear and respect.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Forum's Official's Joker and Trickster. Pardon my bad english, aint my first language (I Speak Spanish).<br /> ST: DtF, HtR, WtO, MtA<br /> Signature Chars: Crowley (hakalu), Joe The Nuwisha (WtA)<br /> Changelings: be afraid of the Technocracian High Five of Doom","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1754-crowley","name":"Crowley","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1754_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-31T17:58:20-08:00","id":1024958,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1024365-south-american-magick-traditions-and-so-on?p=1024891#post1024891\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The Cult of Santa Muerte has a HUGE Aztec component and probably moreso in a WOD slant, as does much of the more &quot;death/dead friendly&quot; side of Mexican culture that results in Dia de Muertos, though there are definitely some Mayan, Zapotec, etc. aspects in there as well. If there's a Euthanatos group in there, it's not one that's gotten a lot of attention in the past. Though it might be a really fertile area for one. Or, perhaps some allied Euthanatos, Dreamspeakers, Verbena and maybe Choristers who all borrow from a rich combination of indigenous and European occult lore. It almost seems like it could almost be a Craft of some sort.... like the Carribean melange that results in the Bataa. Not really sure what to do with Jesus Malverde in all that......</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't say Santa Muerte didn't have elements of Aztec belief. I said that White Wolf only ever covered the Aztec/Maya/Inca beliefs <i>as they were in the past</i>. It never had anything to say about contemporary magical beliefs from this region. Which is a shame, given that it is alive, well, and indeed thriving. The cult of Santa Muerte is extensive in the Central America and the southwest US, and centers of worship have broken out elsewhere.<br /> <br /> I do recall there existing Euthanatos factions (or at least Euthanatoi-aligned factions) in that area of the world. They were, again, the aforementioned old school practitioners of the old Central/South American empires, who showed up briefly for the Convocation, displayed some interest in the Chakravanti, and then left, never to be heard from again. They're hinted at still being around, but well hidden.<br /> <br /> My intention was to say that, if practitioners of the modern Santa Muerte worship aren't officially part of the Euthanatos Tradition, they <i>should be</i>. In fact, as part of my Magestuck fanfic, I had one show up as a notable guest star, and she was a Euthanatos.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-31T19:04:11-08:00","id":1024974,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1024365-south-american-magick-traditions-and-so-on?p=1024958#post1024958\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I didn't say Santa Muerte didn't have elements of Aztec belief. I said that White Wolf only ever covered the Aztec/Maya/Inca beliefs <i>as they were in the past</i>. It never had anything to say about contemporary magical beliefs from this region. Which is a shame, given that it is alive, well, and indeed thriving. The cult of Santa Muerte is extensive in the Central America and the southwest US, and centers of worship have broken out elsewhere.<br /> <br /> I do recall there existing Euthanatos factions (or at least Euthanatoi-aligned factions) in that area of the world. They were, again, the aforementioned old school practitioners of the old Central/South American empires, who showed up briefly for the Convocation, displayed some interest in the Chakravanti, and then left, never to be heard from again. They're hinted at still being around, but well hidden.<br /> <br /> My intention was to say that, if practitioners of the modern Santa Muerte worship aren't officially part of the Euthanatos Tradition, they <i>should be</i>. In fact, as part of my Magestuck fanfic, I had one show up as a notable guest star, and she was a Euthanatos.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think there is the &quot;spooky skull woman&quot; = &quot;modern fantasy necromancer&quot; = Euthanatos issue in play. It's been a problem since the very first where Euthanatos = death mage = spooky scary guy = &quot;stereotypical James Bond Voodoo guy&quot; situation. Death magic is really poorly represented in Mage overall. Entropy really doesn't have diddly to do with death. Life and Matter have as much or more. And I don't see a lot of &quot;Fate&quot; emphasis in the cult<br /> <br /> There are some cool death mages archetypes out there, and this would definitely be one awesome group to add to the short list of key core components. Absent the existence of a Sphere or Tradition to really encompass their &quot;thing&quot;, Euthanatos might do it. Mostly since they are effectively the &quot;you creep us out Ex Misc hodgepodge of groups that would otherwise mostly likely be Verbena and do ghost stuff not spirit stuff so they aren't quite Dreamspeakers&quot; of the Nine Traditions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-31T19:31:43-08:00","id":1024980,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1024365-south-american-magick-traditions-and-so-on?p=1024974#post1024974\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think there is the &quot;spooky skull woman&quot; = &quot;modern fantasy necromancer&quot; = Euthanatos issue in play. It's been a problem since the very first where Euthanatos = death mage = spooky scary guy = &quot;stereotypical James Bond Voodoo guy&quot; situation. Death magic is really poorly represented in Mage overall. Entropy really doesn't have diddly to do with death. Life and Matter have as much or more. And I don't see a lot of &quot;Fate&quot; emphasis in the cult<br /> <br /> There are some cool death mages archetypes out there, and this would definitely be one awesome group to add to the short list of key core components. Absent the existence of a Sphere or Tradition to really encompass their &quot;thing&quot;, Euthanatos might do it. Mostly since they are effectively the &quot;you creep us out Ex Misc hodgepodge of groups that would otherwise mostly likely be Verbena and do ghost stuff not spirit stuff so they aren't quite Dreamspeakers&quot; of the Nine Traditions.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can see that. I think, given that much brujeria (Santa Muerte or otherwise) revolves around praying for common shifts in fortune (money, love, justice), Entropy could work well for the cult. Then again, so could Correspondence, because one of Santa Muerte's iconic possessions is the globe. On yet a third hand, the pale lady evolved from an underworld goddess, so Spirit or Entropy could work as their specialty Sphere. In the latter case, Santa Muerte cultists would still fit well with the Tradition that most often ventures into the underworld.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-31T20:05:17-08:00","id":1024987,"title":"","content":"Well, you will have to decide whether you will be using traditional Native American magic or the modern Chilean magic (neither of which has much to do with the Aztec or Mexican magical systems). I would suggest looking into the traditions of the Mapuche people of Chile and Argentina as an example of traditional Native American magic. For modern Chilean magic, you are probably looking at a synthesis of Catholic and traditional Native American beliefs. In addition, it is important to remember that each region of Chile has its own mythology and magical traditions (basically dividing Chile into a Central tradition, an Easter Island tradition, a Northern tradition, and a Southern tradition).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}